fix(browser): make cef_supervision exist wherever it is called

Running cargo test --all on Windows under windows-tests failed to compile:
E0433, cannot find cef_supervision in super. Two conditions described the
same thing and disagreed. The module existed under any(test, native_browser);
the three functions in cef_runtime_policy call it under
any(target_os = "windows", target_os = "macos").

build.rs only sets native_browser for macOS, and for Windows without the
windows-tests feature. So under windows-tests the module survived on cfg(test)
alone — which is why --lib never saw it: --lib compiles the library in test
mode only. --all also builds it in normal mode for the binaries, and there
the module was gone while the calls remained.

The module condition now follows the calls. native_browser is only ever set
on those two systems, so this is wider than before and never narrower.

The CI contract test moves with it: it pinned --lib for the Windows job. What
that test protects is unchanged and still asserted — that job prepares no CEF
and mounts no CEF cache, and without native_browser no binary links
libcef.dll. The reason is written next to the assertion.

Job budgets are also widened. The first attempt sized them on one favourable
run and killed three healthy jobs, including the Linux suite that was green
before. macOS and Windows commonly run 55 minutes and the full suite adds to
that, so budgets now bound runaway jobs rather than arbitrate normal duration.

macOS runs with --test-threads=1: in parallel, the full suite fails one extra
test in app_update_helper_process that passes on its own, so those tests
interfere. Windows already ran single-threaded.
